# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Suno Master Utility — ignore rules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# macOS. Finder writes these into every directory you browse, including the
# repo root. They encode local window geometry and are not source.
.DS_Store

# The other half of the same problem: AppleDouble sidecars (._<name>) get
# created when this tree is copied to or checked out on a non-HFS volume. macOS
# strips them on copy, so one that was committed comes back truncated garbage.
._*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scratchpad/ — NEVER COMMIT THIS.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It holds unpacked copies of third-party Chrome extensions (for behavioural
# comparison only), raw recon captures, and working notes that may quote
# endpoint names, flag lists, or partial response bodies harvested from Suno's
# own servers. None of it is part of this extension's build; none of it should
# ever leave the machine. It is a research bench, not a source tree. It is also
# ~28 MB, most of it unpacked extensions and the extension .zip files they
# came in.
scratchpad/

# Build artefacts. There is no build step in this repo: nothing here is
# generated, minified or bundled by us, so a zip is never an output of
# anything. It is always a hand-made package somebody is about to share by
# accident. (The third-party bundles in vendor/ were not produced here either —
# they ship as source, so nothing legitimate ever needs zipping.)
*.zip

# Not currently a dependency-bearing project — there is no package.json and no
# toolchain. Listed so that adding one cannot quietly create a committed
# node_modules tree.
node_modules/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# vendor/ — TRACKED ON PURPOSE. DO NOT ADD AN IGNORE RULE FOR IT.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This directory holds the two encoders that MP3 and Ogg Vorbis export need,
# and it has to be committed:
#
#     vendor/lame.all.js          lamejs 1.2.1                    (LGPL-3.0)
#     vendor/OggVorbisEncoder.js  higuma/ogg-vorbis-encoder-js    (MIT + Xiph BSD)
#
# Three independent reasons, any one of which is decisive:
#
#   1. They are loaded from inside the extension package at runtime.
#      offscreen/offscreen.js injects a <script src> for each one, resolved
#      relative to offscreen/offscreen.html, so the bytes have to be in the
#      extension directory. loadVendoredScript() asserts the resolved URL's
#      protocol is chrome-extension: and refuses anything else.
#   2. MV3 extension pages run under `script-src 'self'` — see the CSP meta
#      tag in offscreen/offscreen.html — and the Web Store policy bans remotely
#      hosted code outright. Fetching an encoder from a CDN is therefore not an
#      available fallback; there is no "download it if missing" path to degrade
#      to, and adding one would be a policy violation, not a fix.
#   3. An untracked vendor/ fails silently and only in production. The
#      extension still loads, the options page still offers MP3 and Ogg Vorbis,
#      and sunoTranscode just returns ENCODER_UNAVAILABLE: the control looks
#      live, clicks succeed, and no file appears. A fresh clone would ship that
#      way with no error anywhere.
#
# That third point is the whole reason this directory is spelled out here. The
# failure is invisible until someone tries to export audio, which is exactly
# the kind of bug an ignore rule causes rather than prevents.
#
# Licence obligations point the same way — toward tracking. LGPL-3.0 §4 and the
# MIT/Xiph-BSD terms require the licence notices to travel with the library,
# and vendor/README.md records the pinned upstream version, commit and SHA-256
# of both files. So these are committed as a set:
#
#     vendor/lame.all.js
#     vendor/OggVorbisEncoder.js
#     vendor/LICENSE-lamejs.txt
#     vendor/LICENSE-OggVorbisEncoder.txt
#     vendor/README.md
#
# Dropping the .txt licences, or the provenance note that says which upstream
# bytes these are, would break the terms under which the encoders may be
# redistributed. That is an obligation to ship files, so it is an argument for
# tracking vendor/ — never for ignoring it.
#
# Do not minify, re-bundle, patch or concatenate these files. The recorded
# hashes are only meaningful while the bytes match upstream, and vendor/
# remaining a drop-in replacement is precisely what discharges the LGPL
# obligation above.

# Log files. Diagnostics live in chrome.storage.local and are exported through
# the options page; nothing should ever land here.
*.log